@meta
id: 10-17
title: Inside the tag
chapter: 10
type: lesson
skills: obj-tag
par: 64
minutes: 6
vscode: select between > and < without catching either
open: OrderSummary.tsx

@brief
`it` is the object that has no delimiter key of its own, because its
delimiters are words: it means everything between a matching pair of
tags. Vim reads the opening tag, finds its partner — skipping any nested
pairs of the same name on the way — and gives you what is between them.

In JSX that is the visible text of an element, which is exactly the thing
designers keep changing.

Two copy changes came back from the review of the order page:

  - the heading should show the reference, not the word "summary":
    `Order {order.reference}`
  - the button should say `Cancel order`, because on a page full of
    buttons "Cancel" is ambiguous

The markup itself does not change. Same tags, same attributes, same
class names.

@keys
| `it` | everything between the opening and closing tag |
| `cit` | change the contents of the element |
| `at` | the tags themselves as well |
| `dit` | empty an element out |

@hints
The element is the object. You want what is between the tags, not the
tags.
--
`cit` — change inner tag. It works from anywhere between the two tags,
including on the tags themselves.
--
`fO` puts you on the heading text and `fC` on the button label. Then
`cit` and type the new copy.

@solution
    5j fO cit Order {order.reference} <Esc>
    2j 0 fC cit Cancel order <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Fifty-six keystrokes, thirty-four of them the new copy.

`cit` is the reason people who write a lot of JSX get attached to vim. The
markup and the content are tangled together on one line, the content is
the part that changes, and `cit` separates them without you having to aim
at the `>` and the `<`.

It handles nesting properly, which a naive selection does not. With the
cursor on the text inside a `<span>` that sits inside a `<div>`, `cit`
gets the span. Put the cursor on the div's own text and you get the div,
nested span and all. And like the bracket objects, it takes a count:
`c2it` is the enclosing element, one level out.

One caveat worth knowing: it is a text object, not a parser. A self-closing
`<Badge />` has no pair, so `it` has nothing to find and will beep at you.

@start OrderSummary.tsx
import { formatDate } from '../format';

export function OrderSummary({ order }: Props) {
  return (
    <section className="order-summary">
      <h2>Order summary</h2>
      <p>Placed on {formatDate(order.placedAt)}</p>
      <button type="button">Cancel</button>
    </section>
  );
}

@expected OrderSummary.tsx
import { formatDate } from '../format';

export function OrderSummary({ order }: Props) {
  return (
    <section className="order-summary">
      <h2>Order {order.reference}</h2>
      <p>Placed on {formatDate(order.placedAt)}</p>
      <button type="button">Cancel order</button>
    </section>
  );
}
